          "lead": "Six qualifications apply to this model. Each is stated in full and stays open; none changes the bottom line on its own.",
          "items": [
            {
              "title": "EBITDA is the company’s own measure",
              "label": "Earnings",
              "text": "EBITDA as defined adds back items the company chooses. No independent adjustment was made, so leverage on a stricter measure would be higher.",
              "trace": "#/library/transdigm/2025-06-30/evidence"
            },
            {
              "title": "FY23 cash interest is missing",
              "label": "Data",
              "text": "Cash interest for FY23 was not split out in the sources. Interest cover and free cash flow for that year are shown as n.a., not estimated.",
              "trace": "#/library/transdigm/2025-06-30/evidence"
            },
            {
              "title": "FY22 inventories are missing",
              "label": "Data",
              "text": "The FY22 balance sheet in the sources does not show inventories separately. The figure is n.a. and FY22 working capital ratios are not stated.",
              "trace": "#/library/transdigm/2025-06-30/evidence"
            },
            {
              "title": "No acquisitions or shareholder returns after FY25",
              "label": "Forecast",
              "text": "Both cases assume no acquisitions and forecast no dividends or buybacks. The company has made both in most years, so cash and leverage are likely to be worse than shown.",
              "trace": "#/library/transdigm/2025-06-30/evidence"
            },
            {
              "title": "The downside case is a single scenario",
              "label": "Downside case",
              "text": "One traffic shock was modelled. It does not combine lower traffic with a loss of pricing power, and it assumes the 2028 maturities can still be refinanced.",
              "trace": "#/library/transdigm/2025-06-30/evidence"
            },
